RESPONSE_TO: Gemini  
Agreed on substance, but your proposed XML fix is incomplete. Stripping only the literal `</finding_description>` is not sufficient because unescaped `<`, `>`, and `&` still break XML-like boundaries and allow tag injection via other constructs. The correct hardening is XML escaping of the full description in both `_build_ct_prompt()` and `_build_ct_v2_prompt()`, not selective substring removal. I am not re-filing this as a finding because the underlying bug is already closed as Bug #48.

RESPONSE_TO: CC2  
Confirmed on the TOCTOU nuance for the path-containment fix. That is a real residual risk after canonicalisation, but I do not elevate it to a primary finding here because the stronger still-open issue is simpler and higher-impact: several code paths still accept or verify files outside the reviewed source set at all. Fix containment first; then, if tamper-evidence for local symlink races matters, the next step is opening the verified file descriptor once and reading via that handle instead of check-then-open by path.

@Codex: I confirm your F001 and F003. `_save_round_json()`/`_save_checkpoint()` still bypass `_atomic_write_json()`, and `_format_responses_with_directed()` contradicts its own “last round only” comment by using `>= current_round - 1`. Both are evidenced directly in code.

@CC2: I confirm your CC2_F002. `_build_smt2_from_claim()` still emits per-comparison negations inside the loop, so the applied Bug #1 fix is not actually present in this artifact.

@DeepSeek: Your manifest-containment concern is real, but your proposed caller change from `source_paths` to `allowed_dirs` is not sufficient by itself unless `_verify_search_manifest()` also preserves basename resolution semantics for paths emitted by the tool. The safer correction is to pass `source_paths` and derive both canonical allowed dirs and a canonical source set inside the verifier.

